---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { withBase } from '../../lib/urls';

const appStoreUrl = 'https://apps.shopify.com/blocklist';
---

<BaseLayout
	title="Blox vs Blockify: blocklist-first vs fraud scoring"
	description="Compare Blox and Blockify for Shopify fraud prevention: community customer blocklist and identity linking vs IP, bot, and country-style fraud filtering."
>
	<article class="section compare">
		<div class="site-wrap compare__wrap">
			<span class="section-label">Comparison</span>
			<h1>Blox vs Blockify: blocklist-first vs fraud scoring</h1>
			<p class="section-lead">
				Merchants searching for Shopify <strong>fraud prevention</strong> often compare a
				<em>customer blocklist</em> to a <em>fraud filter</em>. Here’s a clear head-to-head.
			</p>

			<section class="compare__block" aria-labelledby="positioning">
				<h2 id="positioning">The short version</h2>
				<p>
					<strong>Blox</strong> is blocklist-first: every identifier a customer uses is grouped
					into one profile automatically, so blocking fraudsters, chargeback abusers, serial
					returners, and refund abusers takes one action, and that decision is shared across a
					merchant network.
				</p>
				<p>
					<strong>Blockify</strong> is commonly used as a fraud-filtering / blocking toolkit
					oriented around signals like IP, bots, and country-style controls (plus a large App
					Store footprint). It’s closer to “score and filter the session” than “remember this
					customer forever across stores.”
				</p>
				<p>
					If your pain is <strong>repeat chargebacks</strong> and multi-account abuse, start
					with a blocklist. If your pain is bots and geo/IP noise, a filter-first app may be
					the better primary tool, or use both.
				</p>
			</section>

			<section class="compare__block" aria-labelledby="table-heading">
				<h2 id="table-heading">Side-by-side</h2>
				<div class="compare__table-wrap">
					<table>
						<thead>
							<tr>
								<th>Dimension</th>
								<th>Blox</th>
								<th>Blockify (typical use)</th>
							</tr>
						</thead>
						<tbody>
							<tr>
								<td>Primary model</td>
								<td>Community customer blocklist with automatic identity linking</td>
								<td>Fraud filtering / blockers (IP, bot, country, etc.)</td>
							</tr>
							<tr>
								<td>Best against</td>
								<td>Chargebacks, serial returners, refund abuse, duplicate accounts</td>
								<td>Bots, unwanted geos, session-level abuse patterns</td>
							</tr>
							<tr>
								<td>Decision style</td>
								<td>Explicit block; simple rules; network flags for review</td>
								<td>Filter / score / block traffic patterns</td>
							</tr>
							<tr>
								<td>Cross-store memory</td>
								<td>Yes: when one store flags fraud, others can be protected</td>
								<td>Not the core product story</td>
							</tr>
							<tr>
								<td>Shopify focus</td>
								<td>
									<a href={appStoreUrl} rel="noopener noreferrer">Blox on the App Store</a>
								</td>
								<td>Large installed base; filter-oriented positioning</td>
							</tr>
						</tbody>
					</table>
				</div>
			</section>

			<section class="compare__block" aria-labelledby="choose">
				<h2 id="choose">Which should you choose?</h2>
				<ul>
					<li>
						<strong>Choose Blox</strong> if you need to stop the same person coming back after
						a chargeback or refund abuse, especially with new emails or accounts.
					</li>
					<li>
						<strong>Choose a filter-first app like Blockify</strong> if your top issues are bots,
						IP ranges, or country blocking.
					</li>
					<li>
						<strong>Use both</strong> when you want session filtering <em>and</em> durable
						customer bans with network context.
					</li>
				</ul>
			</section>

			<section class="compare__block" aria-labelledby="faq">
				<h2 id="faq">FAQ</h2>
				<h3>Can Blox automatically cancel orders from blocked customers?</h3>
				<p>
					<strong>Yes.</strong> Blox cancels or holds high-risk orders before fulfillment,
					including customers who match your blocklist or network risk signals, so you aren’t
					shipping into known abuse. Set holds vs auto-cancels to match your risk tolerance.
				</p>
			</section>

			<p class="compare__cta">
				<a class="btn" href={appStoreUrl} rel="noopener noreferrer">Install Blox on Shopify</a>
			</p>
			<p class="compare__more">
				<a href={withBase('guides/how-to-block-repeat-chargeback-customers/')}>How to block repeat chargeback customers</a>
				·
				<a href={withBase('guides/blocklist-vs-fraud-filter/')}>Blocklist vs fraud filter</a>
			</p>
		</div>
	</article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
	.compare {
		padding-top: 3.5rem;
		padding-bottom: 5rem;
	}

	.compare__wrap {
		max-width: 46rem;
	}

	.compare h1 {
		font-size: clamp(2rem, 4vw, 2.85rem);
		max-width: 18ch;
		margin-bottom: 1rem;
	}

	.compare__block {
		margin-top: 2.75rem;
	}

	.compare__block h2 {
		font-size: 1.45rem;
		margin-bottom: 0.85rem;
	}

	.compare__block h3 {
		font-size: 1.1rem;
		margin: 1.5rem 0 0.55rem;
	}

	.compare__block p,
	.compare__block li {
		color: var(--color-fg-muted);
	}

	.compare__block p {
		margin: 0.85rem 0;
	}

	.compare__block ul {
		margin: 0.75rem 0 0;
		padding-left: 1.2rem;
	}

	.compare__block li {
		margin: 0.55rem 0;
	}

	.compare__block strong {
		color: var(--color-fg);
	}

	.compare__table-wrap {
		overflow-x: auto;
		margin-top: 1rem;
		border: 1px solid var(--color-border);
	}

	table {
		width: 100%;
		border-collapse: collapse;
		font-size: 0.95rem;
	}

	th,
	td {
		padding: 0.85rem 1rem;
		text-align: left;
		vertical-align: top;
		border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border);
	}

	th {
		font-family: var(--font-display);
		font-size: 0.85rem;
		letter-spacing: -0.02em;
		background: var(--color-bg-elevated);
	}

	td {
		color: var(--color-fg-muted);
	}

	tr:last-child td {
		border-bottom: 0;
	}

	.compare__cta {
		margin-top: 2.5rem;
	}

	.compare__more {
		margin-top: 1.25rem;
		color: var(--color-fg-muted);
		font-size: 0.95rem;
	}

	.compare__more a {
		color: var(--color-fg);
		text-decoration: none;
	}

	.compare__more a:hover {
		color: var(--color-red);
	}
</style>
